# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Compiler
#
# GCC, by an ABSOLUTE path, and this is deliberate on both counts.
#
# Absolute: Fedora puts ccache shims (/usr/lib64/ccache) ahead of gcc on PATH.
# The shims cannot write to ~/.cache/ccache inside Bazel's read-only sandbox,
# so every compile fails with "Read-only file system". Bazel keeps its own
# action cache, so ccache buys nothing here anyway. Verified 2026-08-25.
#
# GCC rather than clang: orca pins clang, and copying that here would be wrong.
# Phase 3 compares the Bazel-built compiler against the Make-built one, and the
# Makefile uses gcc. Pinning clang would change the build system AND the
# compiler in one step, so any behavioural difference would have two candidate
# causes and neither could be attributed.
#
# Clang is still built and tested, via --config=clang. The sanitizer configs
# added in phase 6 select it explicitly, because this machine's gcc has a
# broken libasan (a linker script with no matching .so).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
build --repo_env=CC=/usr/bin/gcc
build:clang --repo_env=CC=/usr/bin/clang

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Language standard. Matches Makefile:23.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
build --copt=-std=c23

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Warnings. Matches Makefile:23's -Wall -Wextra. Vendored or generated code is
# exempted per-target with copts = ["-w"], never by relaxing this set.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
build --copt=-Wall
build --copt=-Wextra

test --test_output=errors

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Header layering.
#
# Two different mechanisms, and only one of them needs this config.
#
# Bazel's INPUT SANDBOX already refuses a header that no transitive dep
# supplies -- it is simply not in the sandbox, and the compile dies with
# "file not found". That works under gcc, needs no feature, and arrives free
# with strict_hdrs = True (tools/defs.bzl).
#
# LAYERING_CHECK catches the other half: a header that IS present through a
# transitive dep but is not a DIRECT one. Measured 2026-08-30 on
# terminating_stmt.c reaching for ast.h, which types_h supplies transitively:
# exit 0 without the feature, exit 1 with it, naming the module.
#
# CLANG ONLY, also measured. The gcc toolchain does not declare the feature and
# Bazel drops the request silently, so the deliberate defect in
# //testing/layering BUILDS clean under the default config. An exit 0 from
# --features=layering_check on gcc is not evidence of anything.
#
# Scope: only targets with strict_hdrs = True are covered. Every other target
# still takes //include:headers, which exports all 79 headers and makes the
# check vacuous for it. tools/verify_layering.sh reports the covered count.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
build:layering --config=clang
build:layering --features=layering_check

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sanitizers. See docs/SANITIZERS.md for the recorded red/green result of each
# config, and tools/verify_sanitizers.sh for the check that produces it.
#
# CLANG, not the gcc this file pins by default. Measured 2026-08-30:
# /usr/bin/gcc on this machine cannot link -fsanitize=address at all --
# "cannot find /usr/lib64/libasan.so.8.0.0". Clang has the runtimes.
#
# src/runtime IS EXCLUDED, and the exclusion is load-bearing.
# src/codegen/codegen.c hardcodes `gcc` as the linker for a compiled Goo
# program and passes no sanitizer flag (grep -c fsanitize: 0). An instrumented
# libgoo_runtime.a would therefore fail that link with undefined __asan_*
# symbols, and all 76 probe tests would go red for a reason that is not a
# defect. The compiler itself IS instrumented, which is the target worth
# having: 570-plus allocation sites.
#
# THE EXCLUSION IS IN src/runtime/BUILD, not here, as NO_SANITIZE_COPTS on
# each target. --per_file_copt was tried first and abandoned: its regex is
# matched against a string this build does not document, and three plausible
# patterns silently instrumented src/runtime anyway while a fourth
# over-matched src/codegen/runtime_integration.c. A per-target copt is
# checkable by reading the BUILD file, and `-fno-sanitize=all` comes after the
# global flag so it wins.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
build:san-base --repo_env=CC=/usr/bin/clang
build:san-base --copt=-fno-omit-frame-pointer
build:san-base --copt=-g
build:san-base --strip=never
test:san-base --test_output=errors
# poison_test reads freed memory on purpose; asan reports exactly that.
test:san-base --test_tag_filters=-nosan

build:asan --config=san-base
build:asan --copt=-fsanitize=address
build:asan --linkopt=-fsanitize=address

# LEAK DETECTION IS OFF, and the number behind that decision is recorded here.
# Measured 2026-08-30, compiling examples/hello_world.goo with an asan-built
# compiler: "1928 byte(s) leaked in 38 allocation(s)", with ZERO frames in
# LLVM -- every one is goolang's own (type_checker.c, goo.c, types.c,
# ast_constructors.c, parser.y). That is the documented AST-free-leak class,
# not noise from an uninstrumented dependency.
#
# So the leaks are REAL and they are not this gate's job. A leak is not a
# memory-safety violation: asan is here for use-after-free, buffer overflow
# and double free, which are. Turning leak detection on today would make all
# 76 probe tests red for a defect class that already has its own gate
# (ast-free-leak-probe) and its own baseline. Give leaks a floor of their own
# before switching this on, or the gate reports the same thing every run.
test:asan --test_env=ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=0
run:asan --run_under="env ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=0"

build:ubsan --config=san-base
build:ubsan --copt=-fsanitize=undefined
build:ubsan --copt=-fno-sanitize-recover=all
build:ubsan --linkopt=-fsanitize=undefined

# TSan needs every linked object instrumented to avoid false negatives, and
# src/runtime is excluded above for the linker reason. So this config sees the
# compiler's own threading only, which today is none: bin/goo is single
# threaded. Kept as a named config because the runtime's scheduler is where a
# race would live, and closing the codegen link-flag gap is what would let it
# cover that. Do not read a green tsan run as evidence about goroutines.
build:tsan --config=san-base
build:tsan --copt=-fsanitize=thread
build:tsan --linkopt=-fsanitize=thread
